WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:10.462
This is Chapter 1: the non-person
identity inventory, as code.

4
00:00:10.462 --> 00:00:14.804
No account can be given an owner, a
decision or a new credential until it's on

5
00:00:14.804 --> 00:00:19.288
one list, and Microsoft's own commands
give you several lists that don't agree.

6
00:00:19.288 --> 00:00:24.466
We'll run Learn's commands and export script
against stand-ins, see what they miss,

7
00:00:24.466 --> 00:00:27.683
then build one register
with owners, credentials,

8
00:00:27.683 --> 00:00:31.213
permissions and risk, and walk
two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:31.213 --> 00:00:36.730
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.093
Why start with an inventory?

11
00:00:42.093 --> 00:00:45.423
Because everything else in this
project acts on an account,

12
00:00:45.423 --> 00:00:48.005
and you can't act on an
account you haven't found.

13
00:00:48.005 --> 00:00:52.633
The plan's first gate asks for every
service account, app registration,

14
00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:56.384
service principal and managed
identity in one register,

15
00:00:56.384 --> 00:00:59.017
with a type, a risk
and a candidate target.

16
00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:03.231
The plan's critical path runs through
ownership, and an owner has to be

17
00:01:03.231 --> 00:01:07.667
found before anything is touched, so
this chapter and the next come first.

18
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:10.064
The trouble is that
Microsoft's own commands

19
00:01:10.064 --> 00:01:12.700
give you several lists,
and they don't agree.

20
00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:17.813
Next, which cloud.

21
00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:21.044
This estate is a GCC tenant
with commercial Azure,

22
00:01:21.044 --> 00:01:24.195
so every host the read
touches is a worldwide one:

23
00:01:24.195 --> 00:01:29.238
the worldwide Graph endpoint and the
commercial Resource Manager endpoint.

24
00:01:29.238 --> 00:01:33.107
GCC High and the Defense Department
clouds have their own hosts,

25
00:01:33.107 --> 00:01:37.851
and the Graph pages this chapter relies
on list the government service levels.

26
00:01:37.851 --> 00:01:41.258
The code never guesses: you
name the cloud explicitly,

27
00:01:41.258 --> 00:01:43.350
and it refuses one it doesn't know.

28
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.771
The directory reads work the same way,
going only to the domain you name,

29
00:01:47.771 --> 00:01:51.480
never to whatever domain the
machine happens to be in.

30
00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.080
So what does the register have to hold?

31
00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:01.677
On the directory side, Microsoft Learn's
service account documentation gives the

32
00:02:01.677 --> 00:02:04.651
types: the traditional user
account used as a service,

33
00:02:04.651 --> 00:02:07.287
the managed service
account tied to one server,

34
00:02:07.287 --> 00:02:11.613
the group managed service account shared
by several, and on newer domains the

35
00:02:11.613 --> 00:02:16.142
delegated managed service account, which
takes over from a traditional account.

36
00:02:16.142 --> 00:02:19.524
Learn warns that nothing marks
a service account as one,

37
00:02:19.524 --> 00:02:21.730
so its search criteria can miss some.

38
00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:26.125
On the Entra side, the list is always
longer than the apps anyone asked for,

39
00:02:26.125 --> 00:02:30.950
and each service principal carries a
type that tells you what it really is.

40
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:39.108
Put together, the register reads two
collections from Active Directory,

41
00:02:39.108 --> 00:02:41.900
nine from Graph, and one
from Resource Manager.

42
00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.063
The slide shows each read
and why it was chosen.

43
00:02:45.063 --> 00:02:46.844
The pattern is worth noticing:

44
00:02:46.844 --> 00:02:51.549
one server-side query for the user accounts
instead of reading every user three

45
00:02:51.549 --> 00:02:55.042
times, owners and credentials
read where they actually live,

46
00:02:55.042 --> 00:02:58.179
on the app registration, and
Graph's own app roles read

47
00:02:58.179 --> 00:03:01.387
so a permission shows up by
name rather than as a number.

48
00:03:01.387 --> 00:03:05.172
And one read that people forget:
a user-assigned identity's

49
00:03:05.172 --> 00:03:09.460
federated credentials live in
Resource Manager, not in Graph.

50
00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.447
Here's an important line between
Microsoft and the program.

51
00:03:16.447 --> 00:03:20.748
Microsoft tells you how to find accounts;
it doesn't tell you which ones are risky.

52
00:03:20.748 --> 00:03:25.470
The risk rules, the candidate target and
the stale threshold are the workbook's

53
00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.231
design guidance, and the
code keeps them in one

54
00:03:28.231 --> 00:03:31.573
configuration file and labels
them as design guidance.

55
00:03:31.573 --> 00:03:36.168
And notice why stale is only ever
a lead: the last logon attribute

56
00:03:36.168 --> 00:03:41.023
isn't updated on every sign-in, so an
account that looks idle may not be.

57
00:03:41.023 --> 00:03:45.930
The plan says to confirm against the
sign-in logs before retiring anything.

58
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.567
Now Microsoft's starting point.

59
00:03:51.567 --> 00:03:55.525
Learn's service account series is
Microsoft's procedure for this read:

60
00:03:55.525 --> 00:03:58.050
a few PowerShell commands
for user accounts,

61
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.916
managed service accounts
and service principals,

62
00:04:00.916 --> 00:04:03.713
a page that says what to
record about each account,

63
00:04:03.713 --> 00:04:08.080
and a script that exports every app
registration's secrets and certificates.

64
00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.627
Those commands live only inside web pages,
so the repository pins the pages' source,

65
00:04:13.627 --> 00:04:18.180
unchanged, with hashes a test checks
and a weekly watch for changes.

66
00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:20.957
A harness then runs them
exactly as published,

67
00:04:20.957 --> 00:04:23.880
against stand-ins built
from a fictional estate.

68
00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.870
Nothing is installed,
and nothing signs in.

69
00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.995
This is where running
the samples pays off.

70
00:04:32.995 --> 00:04:37.407
On the directory side, the criteria
are right, so the code keeps them.

71
00:04:37.407 --> 00:04:40.500
But the samples read
every user three times and

72
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.671
give you three overlapping
lists, not a register.

73
00:04:43.671 --> 00:04:46.833
The never-expires query
happily returns a person.

74
00:04:46.833 --> 00:04:49.495
The delegation query returns servers.

75
00:04:49.495 --> 00:04:52.504
Nothing reads last logon
or group membership,

76
00:04:52.504 --> 00:04:55.433
so you can't compute
stale or over-privileged.

77
00:04:55.433 --> 00:04:59.011
And a delegated managed service
account comes back unlabeled,

78
00:04:59.011 --> 00:05:01.833
while the group managed
filter drops it entirely.

79
00:05:01.833 --> 00:05:04.880
Each row on the slide is pinned by a test.

80
00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:11.747
On the Entra side, the export
script is a good model

81
00:05:11.747 --> 00:05:15.093
for the credential columns,
so the code keeps it as one.

82
00:05:15.093 --> 00:05:19.768
But the multitenant filter it suggests
actually returns single-tenant apps too.

83
00:05:19.768 --> 00:05:24.218
The script stops to ask for an output
path, so it can't run on a schedule.

84
00:05:24.218 --> 00:05:27.056
It makes extra calls per
app and per credential,

85
00:05:27.056 --> 00:05:30.842
never counts owners, and misses
an app with no credential at all.

86
00:05:30.842 --> 00:05:34.647
And it ignores federated
credentials, managed identities,

87
00:05:34.647 --> 00:05:38.530
permissions and consent, which
is most of what the gate needs.

88
00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:43.580
So the verdict: use Learn's commands as
the definition, not as the register.

89
00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:48.755
So what does the code build?

90
00:05:48.755 --> 00:05:52.768
It starts with one reader that every
chapter in this workbook shares.

91
00:05:52.768 --> 00:05:56.553
Its script blocks are the only
things that touch the network,

92
00:05:56.553 --> 00:05:59.355
a live one for real runs
and a fake one for labs.

93
00:05:59.355 --> 00:06:01.572
And it enforces five simple rules:

94
00:06:01.572 --> 00:06:05.849
it makes only the calls the
configuration declares, it only reads,

95
00:06:05.849 --> 00:06:08.779
it sends the token only
to the cloud's own host,

96
00:06:08.779 --> 00:06:13.055
it checks it's signed in to the right
tenant, and it reads every page.

97
00:06:13.055 --> 00:06:18.200
The fake hands back tiny pages on purpose,
so paging gets tested everywhere.

98
00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:26.133
The register gives every identity
one row with the same columns,

99
00:06:26.133 --> 00:06:28.975
whatever its type, so it
all lands in one file.

100
00:06:28.975 --> 00:06:34.158
Directory accounts carry password age,
last logon and privileged groups;

101
00:06:34.158 --> 00:06:38.237
Entra objects carry owners,
credentials and permissions.

102
00:06:38.237 --> 00:06:42.837
An app's permissions are counted once,
so nothing high risk is counted twice.

103
00:06:42.837 --> 00:06:46.481
Then the program's rules apply:
high, medium or low risk,

104
00:06:46.481 --> 00:06:50.951
and a candidate target from the
workbook's migration path matrix.

105
00:06:50.951 --> 00:06:55.350
That target is only a candidate,
Chapter 3 makes the decision.

106
00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:59.420
And SQL Server accounts are
handed to the Data Platform plan.

107
00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:06.604
The register then becomes
findings, nine rules,

108
00:07:06.604 --> 00:07:11.270
each with a stable ID so the same
gap keeps its name from run to run.

109
00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:14.595
Only one is a hard finding:
a high-risk identity.

110
00:07:14.595 --> 00:07:18.357
The rest are warnings to act on,
like a stale lead to confirm,

111
00:07:18.357 --> 00:07:21.492
an app with fewer than two
owners, a client secret,

112
00:07:21.492 --> 00:07:26.195
consent granted for everyone, or an
account that might really be a person.

113
00:07:26.195 --> 00:07:31.563
The run writes a manifest with the tenant,
every request and a hash of every file,

114
00:07:31.563 --> 00:07:35.445
and Chapter 2 refuses a register
whose hash doesn't match.

115
00:07:35.445 --> 00:07:38.750
All of it is covered by
tests that need no network.

116
00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:43.562
Who is this for?

117
00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:48.288
Developers build the read path, the
identity and directory engineers run it,

118
00:07:48.288 --> 00:07:50.538
and the security officers review it.

119
00:07:50.538 --> 00:07:54.063
Nobody needs a tenant or a
domain until the second lab.

120
00:07:54.063 --> 00:07:58.633
What it may read is short: an ordinary
domain user's read on the directory,

121
00:07:58.633 --> 00:08:01.963
two Graph permissions and
Reader on the identities.

122
00:08:01.963 --> 00:08:06.810
One choice deserves attention: listing
delegated consent grants needs the

123
00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:11.274
broad directory read, and that's the
only reason the inventory holds it.

124
00:08:11.274 --> 00:08:13.643
Without permission to read users,

125
00:08:13.643 --> 00:08:17.720
owners come back as bare IDs,
which is all a count needs.

126
00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:27.670
Lab 1 needs only a laptop with PowerShell
7 and Pester: no tenant, no domain.

127
00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:30.715
First, run Learn's own
samples against the stand-ins,

128
00:08:30.715 --> 00:08:34.340
and read the test for the
delegated managed service account:

129
00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:37.820
Microsoft's command returns it,
and nothing says what it is.

130
00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:42.333
Second, list what the inventory needs,
and notice that nothing can write.

131
00:08:42.333 --> 00:08:45.945
Third, run the inventory
over the fictional estate.

132
00:08:45.945 --> 00:08:49.216
You get thirty-five identities,
eight of them high risk,

133
00:08:49.216 --> 00:08:54.158
a set of findings and warnings, and three
accounts handed to the Data Platform plan.

134
00:08:54.158 --> 00:08:55.970
Next, we read them.

135
00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:03.082
Reading the high-risk rows is
where the rules become real.

136
00:09:03.082 --> 00:09:06.468
Two accounts have passwords
that haven't changed in years;

137
00:09:06.468 --> 00:09:10.912
others sit in powerful groups or are
trusted for unconstrained delegation;

138
00:09:10.912 --> 00:09:13.733
one app holds a directory-wide
write permission.

139
00:09:13.733 --> 00:09:18.620
One account is also a stale lead, so
the note says to verify use first.

140
00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:20.213
And one looks like a person,

141
00:09:20.213 --> 00:09:24.195
so it's flagged to confirm rather
than treated as a service account.

142
00:09:24.195 --> 00:09:28.070
Then you watch the reader refuse an
undeclared call and a cloud name

143
00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:32.180
in the wrong case, and you see who's
handed to the Data Platform plan.

144
00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:41.592
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant and a test
domain, never production, and it only reads.

145
00:09:41.592 --> 00:09:45.383
You register the app in the tenant's
own cloud with a certificate,

146
00:09:45.383 --> 00:09:50.318
grant exactly the two permissions, and give
it Reader where one test identity lives.

147
00:09:50.318 --> 00:09:53.943
Then you run it from a domain-joined
host as an ordinary user.

148
00:09:53.943 --> 00:09:57.276
The point of this lab is to check
what the permissions assumed:

149
00:09:57.276 --> 00:10:01.919
that federated credentials are actually
readable, that a delegated managed service

150
00:10:01.919 --> 00:10:06.430
account is recognized, and that an ordinary
user can read the directory attributes.

151
00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.830
Then cross-check the admin
center, and back it all out.

152
00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.055
Each of these dangers
has a guard in the code.

153
00:10:16.055 --> 00:10:20.191
The two that hurt people most: a
person mistaken for a service account,

154
00:10:20.191 --> 00:10:24.617
which the orphan rule could disable,
and a stale account retired on a guess,

155
00:10:24.617 --> 00:10:27.592
which breaks the job that
only runs once a quarter.

156
00:10:27.592 --> 00:10:32.629
The code flags the first, and never disables
or deletes anything for the second.

157
00:10:32.629 --> 00:10:37.531
Others are quieter: a broad permission
granted just to make it work, the wrong

158
00:10:37.531 --> 00:10:40.021
cloud, throttling on credential reads,

159
00:10:40.021 --> 00:10:43.055
and the register itself
as a map for an attacker.

160
00:10:43.055 --> 00:10:46.590
Notice the guards are rules
and tests, not memory.

161
00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:51.690
Here's what done looks like.

162
00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:56.596
On the register side: every account and
every app in the test estate appears exactly

163
00:10:56.596 --> 00:10:59.867
once, anything that might be
a person has been confirmed,

164
00:10:59.867 --> 00:11:04.205
every high-risk row is lined up for the
first wave, every stale lead has been

165
00:11:04.205 --> 00:11:08.827
checked against the logs, and the database
accounts have gone to their own plan.

166
00:11:08.827 --> 00:11:12.861
On the code side: exactly two Graph
permissions with a certificate,

167
00:11:12.861 --> 00:11:17.260
an explicit cloud, hashes that match
what the security officer received,

168
00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.240
and pinned pages still reviewed.

169
00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:24.578
Next, Chapter 2 finds an owner for every
account, and applies the orphan rule.

170
00:11:24.578 --> 00:11:25.740
Thanks for watching.
